WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.689 --> 00:00:10.170
Currently there are 118 elements that we have
discovered and logged on the Periodic table.

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:15.980
Elements are strange things, simply adding
or removing a single proton from the atom's

00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:23.390
nucleus of an element completely changes it
and essentially makes it a new element.

00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:30.610
Here's the weirdest elements from the periodic
table, that seem to defy the laws of physics.

00:00:30.610 --> 00:00:32.320
1.

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:42.280
First up, Gallium is a soft metal that's sometimes
used in electronics.

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:51.210
But it has a unique party trick, it safely
melts in human hands, then resolidifies again

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:56.239
once it drops below its 30 Celsius melting
point.

00:00:56.239 --> 00:01:02.749
In the early 20th century a popular prank
amongst chemists at university was to smelt

00:01:02.749 --> 00:01:09.940
gallium into utensils then watch as their
classmate's knife and fork melted away from

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:12.020
their hands as they ate.

00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:13.020
2.

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:18.580
But if you think a metal becoming a liquid
at room temperature is weird, how about a

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:22.420
metal being a gas at room temperature.

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:27.150
Copernicium is a highly radioactive synthetic
metal.

00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:33.350
It is one of the weirdest and most elusive
elements, because of two reasons.

00:01:33.350 --> 00:01:40.650
Firstly it decays extremely fast, it has an
astonishingly short half-life of just 29 seconds,

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:43.700
so it doesn't stick around for very long.

00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:47.340
And secondly it is a gas at room temperature.

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:54.640
Both these factors make it extremely difficult
for scientists to study, subsequently we still

00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:58.290
don't know very much about this elusive substance.

00:01:58.290 --> 00:01:59.600
3.

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:04.980
Curium is an extremely radioactive substance
that was named after Marie Curie.

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:11.220
Only a few kilograms of curium are produced
each year because it doesn't have very many

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:12.220
uses.

00:02:12.220 --> 00:02:17.870
However it was used on a Mars mission in an
Alpha Proton X-Ray Spectrometer.

00:02:17.870 --> 00:02:25.970
But what makes this element so unique is that
it's so radioactive, it actually glows in

00:02:25.970 --> 00:02:27.040
the dark.

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:28.060
4.

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:34.310
Next up is Francium, a naturally occurring
element named after France, Francium is one

00:02:34.310 --> 00:02:39.190
of the alkali metals, all of which are extremely
volatile.

00:02:39.190 --> 00:02:44.390
But Francium is the most reactive of all the
alkali metals.

00:02:44.390 --> 00:02:52.819
At it's most stable isotope it has a very
short half-life of 22 minutes and is unbelievably

00:02:52.819 --> 00:03:01.580
rare, it usually pops into existence one atom
at a time then disappears almost instantaneously.

00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:07.910
It is estimated that there are only about
20 grams of Francium on the planet at any

00:03:07.910 --> 00:03:09.890
one time.

00:03:09.890 --> 00:03:15.050
Scientists aren't even able to procure a sample
of Francium large enough so it's visible to

00:03:15.050 --> 00:03:20.290
the naked eye, therefore studying it is almost
impossible.

00:03:20.290 --> 00:03:27.060
But we know that when put into water the reaction
would be spectacularly dangerous.

00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:33.450
Considering that the next most reactive alkali
metal is Cesium, which explodes when it touches

00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:34.450
water.

00:03:34.450 --> 00:03:41.260
However Francium is considerably more volatile
than Cesium and just the moisture in the air

00:03:41.260 --> 00:03:45.640
alone would cause Francium to explode spectacularly.

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:47.120
5.

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:53.319
Next up is a group of 8 different elements,
all relatively new to the periodic table.

00:03:53.319 --> 00:03:56.990
They are all manmade, they all have similar
names (Ununbium, Ununtrium, Ununquadium, Ununpentium,

00:03:56.990 --> 00:04:01.300
Ununhexium, Ununseptium, Ununoxium) and were
all discovered in the exact same manner.

00:04:01.300 --> 00:04:07.890
By scientists bombarding atoms of one element
with atoms of another element.

00:04:07.890 --> 00:04:15.460
This separates them into a completely new
element, but for only a fraction of a second.

00:04:15.460 --> 00:04:23.180
The first to be discovered, Ununbium was first
created in 1996 in a lab in Germany.

00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:29.419
None of these elements have been seen for
more than a second and their inclusion on

00:04:29.419 --> 00:04:33.740
the periodic table still remains under investigation.

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:34.919
6.

00:04:34.919 --> 00:04:38.910
Bismuth is the heaviest non radioactive metal.

00:04:38.910 --> 00:04:45.900
Actually it is very very slightly radioactive,
but it has a half life billions of times longer

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:51.370
than the current age of the universe, so for
arguments sake, it's fair to say that it's

00:04:51.370 --> 00:04:53.850
completely stable.

00:04:53.850 --> 00:04:56.760
Bismuth is used in medicine and cosmetics.

00:04:56.760 --> 00:05:01.680
But it has a unique attribute, it is diamagnetic.

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:08.400
Which means it emits a magnetic field in the
opposite direction to any externally applied

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:09.720
magnetic field.

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:15.960
In layman's terms, if you put a magnet between
two lumps of bismuth it will levitate indefinitely.

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:17.110
7.

00:05:17.110 --> 00:05:22.870
You probably imagine sulphur as a yellow rock,
but it can actually change to an array of

00:05:22.870 --> 00:05:26.740
different colours in different conditions.

00:05:26.740 --> 00:05:32.760
One of the weirdest such example can be seen
if you melt sulphur, it turns from a solid

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:36.360
yellow rock to a blood-red liquid.

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:41.810
Also just as cool, if you burn sulphur, it
produces a neon blue flame.

00:05:41.810 --> 00:05:49.330
But sulphur has a disgusting side, it's responsible
for the odour of rotten eggs, garlic, onions

00:05:49.330 --> 00:05:51.530
and many other nasty smells.

00:05:51.530 --> 00:05:56.470
In fact most things that smell bad have some
sulphur in there somewhere.

00:05:56.470 --> 00:05:57.470
8.

00:05:57.470 --> 00:06:03.990
Next up is Helium, it's a colourless, odourless,
tasteless, inert gas, which means it doesn't

00:06:03.990 --> 00:06:06.630
react with other elements.

00:06:06.630 --> 00:06:10.480
Its boiling and melting points are the lowest
of any element.

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:20.510
At an unimaginably low -272.2 degrees Celsius,
Helium turns into a superfluid.

00:06:20.510 --> 00:06:27.090
Which exhibits a unique property, if you pour
helium in its superfluid form into a vessel

00:06:27.090 --> 00:06:33.300
it seems to defy gravity and creep up the
walls of the container and pour over the rim

00:06:33.300 --> 00:06:38.290
to try and reach a warmer place, where it
will eventually evaporate.

00:06:38.290 --> 00:06:47.030
But by far the thing that makes Helium so
damn extraordinary is this.

00:06:47.030 --> 00:06:49.389
9.

00:06:49.389 --> 00:06:57.510
If it wasn't for this next element, you wouldn't
be watching this video right now.

00:06:57.510 --> 00:07:06.180
I am of course talking about silicon, a crucial
component in all computer chips and most electronics.

00:07:06.180 --> 00:07:11.250
It's even got an entire valley named after
it in California.

00:07:11.250 --> 00:07:17.699
It's also responsible for silky white beaches,
being the most common component in sand.

00:07:17.699 --> 00:07:20.960
Oh and don't forget breast implants.

00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:28.750
It's one of the most common elements on earth,
about 27 percent of the earth's crust is made

00:07:28.750 --> 00:07:29.750
up of silicon.

00:07:29.750 --> 00:07:35.930
Silicon is a remarkable element because it's
largely responsible for the technological

00:07:35.930 --> 00:07:39.250
revolution of the past 30 years.

00:07:39.250 --> 00:07:40.250
10.

00:07:40.250 --> 00:07:48.639
And finally, it may not be particularly strange,
but it is arguably the coolest element.

00:07:48.639 --> 00:07:52.229
The element I'm talking about is Carbon.

00:07:52.229 --> 00:07:55.970
It is the most essential element to support
life.

00:07:55.970 --> 00:07:58.819
It is the building block of everything living.

00:07:58.819 --> 00:08:05.300
DNA, proteins, cells, coal, pencils, diamonds,
the air you breath and the food you eat are

00:08:05.300 --> 00:08:09.949
all made up from carbon, as well as countless
other things.

00:08:09.949 --> 00:08:15.310
Carbon compromises 20% of the weight of all
living organisms.

00:08:15.310 --> 00:08:21.039
To put it simply, if it wasn't for carbon
we wouldn't be here, and neither would the

00:08:21.039 --> 00:08:30.500
universe as we know it, so for that reason
alone it deserves some attention.

